    "passage": "Plastic pollution has been widely discussed and brought to the attention of the public these days. Tiny pieces of plastic, or microplastics, are [[1]] detected throughout waterways worldwide. While the exact harms of human ingesting microplastics [[2]] to be investigated by science, most agree it’s a problem that deserves immediate treatment. After all, those fragments of plastic may carry marine pollutants, which may give rise to a host of health [[3]] in the long run.\nHowever, [[4]] as we work on the problem, our current means of removing microplastics from water is potentially toxic in some cases. A team of researchers from Texas hence took on the task of seeking a much safer [[5]], and they ended up with a humble-looking alternative. Okra, which makes a popular side dish or [[6]] in our meal, may be a solution to our plastic pollution. The Texas team found that compounds [[7]] from okra work wonders with both seawater and freshwater. Chemicals from okra seeds form into clumps with particles in the water, [[8]] pollutants in the process. It’s even more promising since there seems to be no need to [[9]] existing facilities or processes with okra. For now, the research has been [[10]] to include a wider variety of water sources. Hopefully, their work can come to fruition and solve the pollution problem soon.",
